{
  "id": "adjektive-nach-sein",
  "name": "Adjectives: a big house, the house is big",
  "level": "A1",
  "status": "entwurf",
  "source": "EnglishAcademy original English text (grammar-a1 run, 2026-10-29)",
  "subtitle": "The kitchen is small. The weather is nice. An adjective never takes -s, and it comes before the noun: a small kitchen. With very, too and not you say more.",
  "briefMarks": [
    "never takes -s",
    "before the noun",
    "very",
    "too",
    "not"
  ],
  "concept": {
    "flow": [
      {
        "text": "the kitchen / the weather"
      },
      {
        "text": "is · looks · gets",
        "variant": "verb"
      },
      {
        "text": "(very / too / not) + adjective",
        "variant": "prep"
      }
    ],
    "exampleHtml": "The kitchen <span class=\"vp-verb\">is</span> <span class=\"vp-prep\">very small</span>. · The weather <span class=\"vp-verb\">is</span> <span class=\"vp-prep\">not nice</span>.",
    "qa": [
      {
        "html": "An adjective <strong>never takes -s</strong>, even before a plural noun. <em>The shoes are red. I have two red cars.</em> Not <em>reds cars</em>.",
        "tag": "No -s"
      },
      {
        "html": "Before a noun, the adjective comes <strong>first</strong>: <em>a small kitchen, a big house</em>. Not <em>a kitchen small</em>. Use <strong>an</strong> if the adjective starts with a vowel sound: <em>an old house</em>, not <em>a old house</em>.",
        "tag": "Before the noun"
      },
      {
        "html": "After <strong>be</strong> (<em>am, is, are</em>) you need the verb and then the adjective: <em>The kitchen is small.</em> Not <em>The kitchen small.</em> It also works after <em>look</em> and <em>get</em>: <em>You look happy. The soup gets cold.</em>",
        "tag": "After be"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>very</strong> makes it stronger: <em>very expensive</em>. <strong>too</strong> means more than you want, a problem: <em>too expensive</em>. <strong>quite</strong> means fairly: <em>The film is quite good.</em> <strong>not</strong> comes after <em>is</em>: <em>The kitchen is not small.</em>",
        "tag": "very · too · quite"
      },
      {
        "html": "✗ <em>I have two reds cars.</em> → ✓ <em>I have two red cars.</em> · ✗ <em>a house big</em> → ✓ <em>a big house</em> · ✗ <em>The kitchen small.</em> → ✓ <em>The kitchen is small.</em> · ✗ <em>a old house</em> → ✓ <em>an old house</em>",
        "tag": "Common mistakes"
      }
    ],
    "noteHtml": "Learn opposites in pairs: <em>big – small, expensive – cheap, old – new, old – young</em>. For people and animals the opposite of <em>old</em> is <em>young</em>. For things it is <em>new</em>. <em>Cheap</em> can sound like bad quality, so for a shop you can say <em>inexpensive</em>. More about word order is at <a href=\"/word-order\">Word order</a>. Next step: <a href=\"/comparatives-superlatives\">bigger and the biggest</a>.",
    "reference": {
      "showLabel": "Show overview ▼",
      "hideLabel": "Hide overview ▲",
